; smf-smpte — a wall-clock division, and the tempo event nobody believes.
;
; 25 frames per second, 40 ticks per frame: OpenMPT's `frames ·
; subframes / 2` makes that a 500-pulse grid, and the file's `set-tempo`
; — 1 000 000 µs, 60 QPM — is kept and recorded as ignored
; (`smpte_tempo_ignored`, plan 03). A SMPTE tick is a slice of the wall
; clock, and the 500-pulse grid reproduces it exactly only when read at
; 120 QPM: that is the approximation's premise (plan 05, "Chunks").
;
; This is one of the corpus's two NON-CANONICAL fixtures, and BOTH rungs
; are pinned `:ok false`, each on purpose:
;
;   rung 1 — the canonical writer's division is ALWAYS pulses per quarter
;   (plan 05, "The canonical writer"), so `write(read(bytes))` differs
;   from these bytes; rung 1 is a gate on files the writer produced, and
;   this file is authored to be one it never would.
;
;   rung 2 — `read(write(read(bytes)))` comes back with every tick, kind
;   and payload but ONE: the set-tempo's. On the converted grid the
;   writer spells every `set-tempo` as 500 000 µs, the tempo the grid was
;   derived for (`Division.tempoOnGrid`), because a 60 QPM believed on a
;   grid derived at 120 would make every second of this file two.
;   `:differs` names exactly that payload and nothing else; any other
;   sentence there is a regression, and so is `:ok true` — it would mean
;   the tempo went through unchanged and the clock with it.
;
; A golden is reviewed, not obeyed: both `:ok false` are read as "the
; writer normalised it, on purpose", and the byte-equality of this whole
; text with `expected.sjon` is the gate.
;
; `(write)` hashes that normalised output — division 500, tempo 500 000
; — so a change in what the writer makes of a SMPTE file moves a number
; rather than a verdict. `(dump)` and `(build :kind mid)` are the
; document half of the same fact: the dumped `midi/` tier carries the
; rewritten event, so the echo's one tempo segment is 500 000 µs and the
; map derived from the dump keeps the wall clock; and the built file's
; hash is `(write)`'s, so `dump → build` and `write` are one answer to
; what this file's grid means.
(read)
(roundtrip)
(write)
(dump)
(build :kind mid)
